      "title": "Malformed suppression",
      "capability": "meta",
      "severity": "error",
      "summary": "A suppression directive names no rule, or tries to suppress everything.",
      "why": "`//gofastr:allow(all)` is a hole, not a decision: it silently absorbs every rule added to the catalog afterwards, including ones written to catch the bug you are about to ship.",
      "fix": "Name each rule explicitly: `//gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1401,GOFASTR1403) \u003creason\u003e`.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "bad": "//gofastr:allow(all) too noisy",
          "good": "//gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1401,GOFASTR1403) reviewed: static SQL, no user input"
        }
      ],
      "doc": "contracts",
      "autofix": false,
      "docUrl": "https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/contracts",
      "docCommand": "gofastr docs contracts",
      "suppress": "//gofastr:allow(GOFASTR0004) \u003creason\u003e"
    }
  ]
}

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1001

GOFASTR1001  Duplicate route registration
routing · routing/duplicate-route · default severity: error

What
  The same method and path are registered more than once.

Why
  The underlying ServeMux panics on a duplicate at registration time, so this
  crashes the app at boot rather than at build. Catching it here turns a
  failed deploy into a failed build, which is the difference between a
  rollback and an edit.

Fix
  Delete one registration, or give them distinct paths. If both are wanted on
  one path, branch inside a single handler.

Example: two handlers claiming POST /orders
  ✗ r.Handle("POST", "/orders", createOrder)
  ✗ r.Handle("POST", "/orders", createOrderV2)
  ✓ r.Handle("POST", "/orders", createOrder)
  ✓ r.Handle("POST", "/v2/orders", createOrderV2)

Docs
  gofastr docs project-structure
  https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/project-structure

Suppress once
  //gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1001) <reason>

Relax project-wide
  contracts:
    rules:
      GOFASTR1001: warn   # or off

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1001 --json
{
  "schema": 1,
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "GOFASTR1001",
      "slug": "routing/duplicate-route",
      "title": "Duplicate route registration",
      "capability": "routing",
      "severity": "error",
      "summary": "The same method and path are registered more than once.",
      "why": "The underlying ServeMux panics on a duplicate at registration time, so this crashes the app at boot rather than at build. Catching it here turns a failed deploy into a failed build, which is the difference between a rollback and an edit.",
      "fix": "Delete one registration, or give them distinct paths. If both are wanted on one path, branch inside a single handler.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "caption": "two handlers claiming POST /orders",
          "bad": "r.Handle(\"POST\", \"/orders\", createOrder)\nr.Handle(\"POST\", \"/orders\", createOrderV2)",
          "good": "r.Handle(\"POST\", \"/orders\", createOrder)\nr.Handle(\"POST\", \"/v2/orders\", createOrderV2)"
        }
      ],
      "doc": "project-structure",
      "autofix": false,
      "docUrl": "https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/project-structure",
      "docCommand": "gofastr docs project-structure",
      "suppress": "//gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1001) \u003creason\u003e"
    }
  ]
}

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1002

GOFASTR1002  Express-style `:param` in a route pattern
routing · routing/colon-path-parameter · default severity: error

What
  A route pattern uses `:name` for a path parameter instead of `{name}`.

Why
  GoFastr routes through net/http's ServeMux, which uses `{name}`. A `:name`
  segment is matched **literally**, so `/users/:id` responds to a request for
  the path `/users/:id` and 404s on `/users/42`. Nothing warns you: the route
  registers fine, the handler compiles fine, and every real request misses it.
  This is the single most common habit carried over from Express, Gin, and
  Chi.

Fix
  Use brace syntax, `/users/{id}`, and read the value with
  `r.PathValue("id")`. For a trailing catch-all, `{path...}`.

Example
  ✗ r.Handle("GET", "/users/:id", showUser)
  ✓ r.Handle("GET", "/users/{id}", showUser)
  ✓ // inside the handler: id := r.PathValue("id")

Docs
  gofastr docs project-structure
  https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/project-structure

Suppress once
  //gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1002) <reason>

Relax project-wide
  contracts:
    rules:
      GOFASTR1002: warn   # or off

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1002 --json
{
  "schema": 1,
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "GOFASTR1002",
      "slug": "routing/colon-path-parameter",
      "title": "Express-style `:param` in a route pattern",
      "capability": "routing",
      "severity": "error",
      "summary": "A route pattern uses `:name` for a path parameter instead of `{name}`.",
      "why": "GoFastr routes through net/http's ServeMux, which uses `{name}`. A `:name` segment is matched **literally**, so `/users/:id` responds to a request for the path `/users/:id` and 404s on `/users/42`. Nothing warns you: the route registers fine, the handler compiles fine, and every real request misses it. This is the single most common habit carried over from Express, Gin, and Chi.",
      "fix": "Use brace syntax, `/users/{id}`, and read the value with `r.PathValue(\"id\")`. For a trailing catch-all, `{path...}`.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "bad": "r.Handle(\"GET\", \"/users/:id\", showUser)",
          "good": "r.Handle(\"GET\", \"/users/{id}\", showUser)\n// inside the handler: id := r.PathValue(\"id\")"
        }
      ],
      "doc": "project-structure",
      "autofix": false,
      "docUrl": "https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/project-structure",
      "docCommand": "gofastr docs project-structure",
      "suppress": "//gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1002) \u003creason\u003e"
    }
  ]
}

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1003

GOFASTR1003  Route has no test
routing · routing/untested-route · default severity: warn

What
  No test file in the module references this route's path.

Why
  An untested route is the one that breaks silently on a refactor. This is the
  static half of the check: cheap, and it runs without a test run. The runtime
  half (GOFASTR1101) proves the route was actually reached.

Fix
  Add a test that exercises the path. `framework.TestHarness(t, app)` gives
  you an in-process client; see `gofastr docs testkit`.

Example
  ✗ r.Handle("GET", "/reports/monthly", monthlyReport) // no test file names this path
  ✓ r.Handle("GET", "/reports/monthly", monthlyReport) // reports_test.go: ta.Get("/reports/monthly")

Docs
  gofastr docs testkit
  https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/testkit

Suppress once
  //gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1003) <reason>

Relax project-wide
  contracts:
    rules:
      GOFASTR1003: warn   # or off

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1003 --json
{
  "schema": 1,
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "GOFASTR1003",
      "slug": "routing/untested-route",
      "title": "Route has no test",
      "capability": "routing",
      "severity": "warn",
      "summary": "No test file in the module references this route's path.",
      "why": "An untested route is the one that breaks silently on a refactor. This is the static half of the check: cheap, and it runs without a test run. The runtime half (GOFASTR1101) proves the route was actually reached.",
      "fix": "Add a test that exercises the path. `framework.TestHarness(t, app)` gives you an in-process client; see `gofastr docs testkit`.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "bad": "r.Handle(\"GET\", \"/reports/monthly\", monthlyReport) // no test file names this path",
          "good": "r.Handle(\"GET\", \"/reports/monthly\", monthlyReport) // reports_test.go: ta.Get(\"/reports/monthly\")"
        }
      ],
      "doc": "testkit",
      "autofix": false,
      "docUrl": "https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/testkit",
      "docCommand": "gofastr docs testkit",
      "suppress": "//gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1003) \u003creason\u003e"
    }
  ]
}

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1004

GOFASTR1004  In-page state modelled as a route
routing · routing/in-page-state-as-route · default severity: info

What
  A route path encodes sort, page, filter, or tab state.

Why
  Sorting a table is not navigation. Routing it costs a full page render,
  loses scroll position and focus, and pushes a history entry the user did not
  ask for. GoFastr models discrete in-page state as islands: the click fires
  an RPC and the runtime swaps one fragment. This is advisory, and only
  checked in modules that render UI: the evidence is a path segment's name,
  and URL-addressable pages are sometimes the point: a blog's page 2 wants a
  URL, and a headless API using `/orders/page/{n}` is just REST. It also only
  speaks to discrete list state. Continuous, client-owned state, like a map
  viewport's lat/lng/zoom, an animated chart's range, or a scrubber position,
  is neither a route nor an island: an RPC per pan would be absurd. That state
  lives in the client signal store, and reflecting it into the URL so the view
  is shareable is a feature, not a finding.

Fix
  For sort/page/filter/tab, move the state into an island, a `core-ui/widget`
  Builder, and let the server return the new island HTML. For continuous state
  (maps, charts), keep it in the client signal store. See `gofastr docs
  interactive-patterns`.

Example: pagination as navigation vs as an island
  ✗ r.Handle("GET", "/orders/page/{n}", ordersPage)
  ✓ r.Handle("GET", "/orders", ordersPage) // the table island handles ?page= internally

Docs
  gofastr docs interactive-patterns
  https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/interactive-patterns

Suppress once
  //gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1004) <reason>

Relax project-wide
  contracts:
    rules:
      GOFASTR1004: warn   # or off

