  "schema": 1,
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "GOFASTR1807",
      "slug": "rendering/hardcoded-token-value",
      "title": "CSS hardcodes a value the theme declares as a token",
      "capability": "rendering",
      "severity": "error",
      "summary": "Design-system CSS or an owned style (*.style.css) sets a property to a literal that is exactly a theme token's value, built in or declared in a \u003cname\u003e.tokens.css.",
      "why": "The design system's promise is that one token swap re-skins every surface. A literal copy of a token's value silently opts out: re-theming --text-xs or --radii-sm leaves the rule behind, and nothing shows the drift, because the rendered pixels are identical until the day someone changes the token. core-ui/widget/theme carried `font-size: 0.75rem` beside a theme declaring --text-xs: 0.75rem for months in exactly this way.",
      "fix": "Reference the token: `font-size: var(--text-xs)` in stylesheet strings, or the `{text.xs}` builder reference in StyleSheet/Set values. An off-scale value no token carries is a MISSING token: add it to the theme instead of hardcoding.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "bad": "ss.Rule(\".eyebrow\").Set(\"font-size\", \"0.75rem\").End()",
          "good": "ss.Rule(\".eyebrow\").Set(\"font-size\", \"{text.xs}\").End()"
        }
      ],
      "doc": "theming",
      "autofix": false,
      "docUrl": "https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/theming",
      "docCommand": "gofastr docs theming",
      "suppress": "//gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1807) \u003creason\u003e"
    }
  ]
}

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1808

GOFASTR1808  var() fallback disagrees with the token it stands in for
rendering · rendering/fallback-drift · default severity: error

What
  Design-system CSS or an owned style (*.style.css) writes `var(--spacing-md,
  12px)` where the theme declares --spacing-md as 8px.

Why
  A themed page resolves the variable, so the fallback never renders there; it
  only shows on a page with no theme CSS. But the number is what the next
  reader learns the token means. Issue #365 found about 450 such fallbacks
  teaching a 4/8/16/24/32 spacing ladder the theme does not declare (it is
  2/4/8/16/24), and a review bot read one of them as the token's value and
  proposed a change that would have shrunk a real gap. The rule covers the
  length and time scales, spacing, radii, text and duration, where a fallback
  can only be right or wrong. In design-system Go code it also covers colour
  fallbacks (--color-* and the --tk-* code colours), which drifted the same
  way: about 350 still named the palette from before the contrast pass
  (`var(--color-danger, #DC2626)` where the theme declares #B91C1C) or a
  translucent rgba() the theme never declares. A colour fallback is the
  token's hex for its scheme (the dark value inside a dark-scheme rule or
  @media block, the light value elsewhere) or a keyword (`currentColor`,
  `inherit`, `transparent`), a deliberate degraded-mode choice. Font stacks
  are left alone, as are owned styles' colours, which follow the app's theme,
  and battery/desktop/ui's, which its own tests check against its per-platform
  theme.

Fix
  Write the declared value as the fallback (`style.TokenNames()` or `gofastr
  docs theming` lists them); rem and px are compared by size, so
  `var(--spacing-lg, 1rem)` is fine for a 16px token, and hex by colour, so
  `#fff` is fine for #FFFFFF. Inside a dark-scheme block write the dark value
  the message names. If the value you wanted is not the token's value, you
  wanted a different token.

Example
  ✗ padding: var(--spacing-md, 12px);
  ✓ padding: var(--spacing-md, 8px);

Docs
  gofastr docs theming
  https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/theming

Suppress once
  //gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1808) <reason>

Relax project-wide
  contracts:
    rules:
      GOFASTR1808: warn   # or off

