=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1501

GOFASTR1501  Regexp compiled inside a function
performance · performance/regexp-compile-per-call · default severity: warn

What
  `regexp.MustCompile` or `regexp.Compile` runs on every call rather than once
  at init.

Why
  Compiling a pattern is orders of magnitude more expensive than matching with
  it. In a request handler this turns a microsecond of work into a
  millisecond, on every request, forever.

Fix
  Hoist it to a package-level `var re = regexp.MustCompile(...)`.

Example
  ✗ func slugify(s string) string {
  ✗     re := regexp.MustCompile(`[^a-z0-9]+`)
  ✗     return re.ReplaceAllString(s, "-")
  ✗ }
  ✓ var reSlug = regexp.MustCompile(`[^a-z0-9]+`)
  ✓ 
  ✓ func slugify(s string) string {
  ✓     return reSlug.ReplaceAllString(s, "-")
  ✓ }

Docs
  gofastr docs benchmarks
  https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/benchmarks

Suppress once
  //gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1501) <reason>

Relax project-wide
  contracts:
    rules:
      GOFASTR1501: warn   # or off

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1501 --json
{
  "schema": 1,
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "GOFASTR1501",
      "slug": "performance/regexp-compile-per-call",
      "title": "Regexp compiled inside a function",
      "capability": "performance",
      "severity": "warn",
      "summary": "`regexp.MustCompile` or `regexp.Compile` runs on every call rather than once at init.",
      "why": "Compiling a pattern is orders of magnitude more expensive than matching with it. In a request handler this turns a microsecond of work into a millisecond, on every request, forever.",
      "fix": "Hoist it to a package-level `var re = regexp.MustCompile(...)`.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "bad": "func slugify(s string) string {\n    re := regexp.MustCompile(`[^a-z0-9]+`)\n    return re.ReplaceAllString(s, \"-\")\n}",
          "good": "var reSlug = regexp.MustCompile(`[^a-z0-9]+`)\n\nfunc slugify(s string) string {\n    return reSlug.ReplaceAllString(s, \"-\")\n}"
        }
      ],
      "doc": "benchmarks",
      "autofix": false,
      "docUrl": "https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/benchmarks",
      "docCommand": "gofastr docs benchmarks",
      "suppress": "//gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1501) \u003creason\u003e"
    }
  ]
}

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1502

GOFASTR1502  Database query inside a loop
performance · performance/query-in-loop · default severity: warn

What
  A Query/Exec/Get call sits inside a range or for loop.

Why
  This is the N+1: one query to get the list, then one per row. It is
  invisible with ten rows in development and takes the database down with ten
  thousand in production.

Fix
  Fetch in one query: `?include=` / eager loading for relations, or an `IN
  (…)` batch. See `gofastr docs includes`.

Example
  ✗ for _, o := range orders {
  ✗     o.Customer, _ = repo.GetCustomer(ctx, o.CustomerID)
  ✗ }
  ✓ orders, _ := repo.List(ctx, query.Include("customer"))

Docs
  gofastr docs includes
  https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/includes

Suppress once
  //gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1502) <reason>

Relax project-wide
  contracts:
    rules:
      GOFASTR1502: warn   # or off

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1502 --json
{
  "schema": 1,
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "GOFASTR1502",
      "slug": "performance/query-in-loop",
      "title": "Database query inside a loop",
      "capability": "performance",
      "severity": "warn",
      "summary": "A Query/Exec/Get call sits inside a range or for loop.",
      "why": "This is the N+1: one query to get the list, then one per row. It is invisible with ten rows in development and takes the database down with ten thousand in production.",
      "fix": "Fetch in one query: `?include=` / eager loading for relations, or an `IN (…)` batch. See `gofastr docs includes`.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "bad": "for _, o := range orders {\n    o.Customer, _ = repo.GetCustomer(ctx, o.CustomerID)\n}",
          "good": "orders, _ := repo.List(ctx, query.Include(\"customer\"))"
        }
      ],
      "doc": "includes",
      "autofix": false,
      "docUrl": "https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/includes",
      "docCommand": "gofastr docs includes",
      "suppress": "//gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1502) \u003creason\u003e"
    }
  ]
}

=== gofastr verify --explain GOFASTR1503

GOFASTR1503  Reflection in a request handler
performance · performance/reflection-per-request · default severity: info

What
  A handler calls into `reflect` on the request path.

Why
  Reflection defeats inlining and escape analysis, and its cost lands on every
  request rather than once at startup. The framework reflects at registration
  time for exactly this reason.

Fix
  Move the reflection to init/registration and cache the result, or replace it
  with a generic function.

Example
  ✗ func handler(w http.ResponseWriter, req *http.Request) {
  ✗ 	t := reflect.TypeOf(payload)
  ✗ 	_ = t
  ✗ }
  ✓ var payloadType = reflect.TypeOf(payload) // resolved once at init

Docs
  gofastr docs benchmarks
  https://donaldmurillo.github.io/gofastr/docs/benchmarks

Suppress once
  //gofastr:allow(GOFASTR1503) <reason>

Relax project-wide
  contracts:
    rules:
      GOFASTR1503: warn   # or off

